  ("YANTRA-EPISTEMIC-POISON-ATTACK" . (
    (priority . 7.5)
    (created . 2026-09-01)
    (last-validated . 2026-09-01)
    (status . active)
    (capabilities . (wsm yantra agent-kernel correctness security testing))
    (origin . ecosystem)
    (context . "From docs/YANTRA-EPISTEMIC-POISON-ATTACK-2026-08-31.md (ecosystem): a read-only probe against lib/yantra.my's claims-execution?, run through the local WSM CLI on an isolated fixture, never against a real command. Status recorded there: BROKEN.")
    (description . "lib/yantra.my's valid-final? gates whether an assistant turn may finish without owned tool-result evidence, using claims-execution? (lib/yantra.my:347) to decide whether the turn's text claims a command was executed. claims-execution? is markers-contained? over claim-markers (lib/yantra.my:337, e.g. \"executed\", \"ran \", \"виконав\") -- a bare substring search with no negation, tense, subject, or ownership awareness. Confirmed both directions on an isolated fixture: false positive -- \"the command was not executed\" contains \"executed\" as a substring and is wrongly flagged as an execution claim (would demand tool-result backing for a truthful denial); false negative -- \"I inspected pwd and found /tmp\" makes no literal marker match and is wrongly treated as claiming nothing, even though it implicitly asserts an observed execution result. The existing 10 yantra.rs tests (crates/my-lisp/tests/) pass but do not cover negation, tense, paraphrase, or multi-call ownership -- they were never adversarial against this specific boundary. Add bounded fixtures for each of these four attack classes first (to pin down the actual intended contract honestly, not to guess a fix), then correct claims-execution?/valid-final? against that fixture set. Do not silently widen claim-markers with more substrings -- that is the same failure class, just larger; the fix must reason about negation/tense/ownership structurally or be explicit that it still doesn't and stays a heuristic prefilter. Keep the invalid-completion-nudge behavior (system message asking for real tool evidence) once the detector is trustworthy.")
    (done . nil))))

